# Fixed defect 131's class, 2026-09-29, an annotated binding whose type
# failed. Symptom: `n: 2 @= 3` cost `expected_type` and then
# `expected_binding_symbol` at the same `2`, one mistake and two messages,
# and the value after the `@` was never read, so a mistake inside it waited
# for a second run. Cause: `parse_type` leaves the token it could not read
# under the cursor, and `grammar_expr.annotated` looked for the `@` or `=`
# at that same token. A type that refused leaves the annotation to resume
# at the `@` or `=` its line holds outside the type's brackets, and the
# symbol is named missing only at a token no refusal of the type stands on
# (`parse/annotation.hero`). The last two functions hold the second
# mistakes that are real and still said.

function a_value_for_a_type()
    n : 2  #~ expected_type
    m: 2 @= 3  #~ expected_type
    k: 2 = 3  #~ expected_type
    j: ) @= 3  #~ expected_type
    i: -1 @= 3  #~ expected_type
    h: "x" = "y"  #~ expected_type

function inside_the_type_s_brackets()
    n: [2] @= [3]  #~ expected_type
    m: [2] 3  #~ expected_type

function the_value_is_read()
    n: 2 @= f(3 +)  #~ expected_type expected_expression
    m: [2] = [3, )]  #~ expected_type expected_expression

function the_symbol_past_the_type_is_its_own(k: i64)
    n: (2) 3  #~ expected_function_type expected_binding_symbol
    m: i64 5  #~ expected_binding_symbol

    match k
        0 => j: 2 @= 3  #~ expected_type declaration_in_arm
        _ => print(k)

function main()
    a_value_for_a_type()
